# Line endings
#
# Git stores text with LF and checks it out with the platform's native ending: CRLF in
# the working tree on Windows, LF everywhere else. Without this file that decision comes
# from core.autocrlf, which is a per-machine setting -- so the same commit produced a
# different diff for whoever cloned it, which is exactly the churn this stops.
#
# These attributes win over core.autocrlf, so the behaviour is the same for everyone
# regardless of how their Git is configured.

* text=auto

# The text we actually ship. Redundant under `text=auto` above, but spelled out: a
# migration's line endings are never cosmetic, and a .sql file is the one file type here
# whose bytes another tool reads directly.
*.py      text
*.sql     text
*.md      text
*.txt     text
*.ini     text
*.example text

# Never translate these. A CR inside a CSV cell or a header byte in an image is data
# corruption, not a formatting preference, and git's binary guess is not good enough to
# be trusted on a generated export.
*.csv   -text
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.db    binary
*.bak   binary